       "After the revolution came the concept of the 'citizen' — all French people got equal rights and France became a nation-state.",
       "In 1804 Napoleon Bonaparte became the Emperor of France and introduced the Napoleonic Code (Civil Code of 1804).",
       "The Napoleonic Code abolished privileges based on birth; it guaranteed equality before the law and the right to property.",
       "Napoleon applied these reforms in the conquered territories (Italy, Germany, Switzerland) too — the feudal system broke down and nationalist consciousness grew.",
       "After Napoleon's defeat at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815, Britain, Russia, Prussia and Austria convened the Congress of Vienna.",
       "The Congress of Vienna (1815) was chaired by the Austrian Chancellor, Duke Metternich.",
       "The aim of the Treaty of Vienna was to restore the monarchical order of pre-1789 Europe and to prevent any future French expansion.",
       "Under the treaty, new states were created around France's frontiers — the Netherlands in the north and Genoa added to Piedmont in the south — to check French expansion.",
       "The Vienna settlement was conservative — it tried to suppress democratic and nationalist movements across Europe.",
       "In the July Revolution of 1830, the Bourbon king Charles X of France was overthrown and Louis Philippe became a constitutional monarch.",
       "Inspired by the 1830 revolution, Belgium broke away from the United Kingdom of the Netherlands and became independent.",
       "In 1848, liberal revolutions swept across Europe — the monarchy fell in France and the Second Republic was established.",
       "In 1848, 831 elected representatives of the German regions met at the Frankfurt Parliament and tried to frame a constitution for a united German nation.",
       "The unification of Germany was achieved under the leadership of Otto von Bismarck, the Prussian Chancellor, through his policy of 'blood and iron'.",
       "On 18 January 1871, the Prussian king William I was proclaimed the German Emperor (Kaiser) at the Palace of Versailles.",
       "The unification of Italy had three heroes — the thinker Mazzini, the statesman Cavour and the warrior Garibaldi.",
       "Mazzini spread nationalist ideas through secret organisations like 'Young Italy' (Marseilles) and 'Young Europe' (Berne).",
       "In 1861 Victor Emmanuel II was proclaimed the king of united Italy; Garibaldi's 'Red Shirts' volunteers played a decisive role in liberating southern Italy.",
