      "points": [
       "In 1919, Gandhiji called for a nationwide satyagraha against the Rowlatt Act.",
       "On 13 April 1919, General Dyer ordered firing on an unarmed crowd at Jallianwala Bagh (Amritsar) — hundreds were killed.",
       "The Non-Cooperation Movement began in 1920; joining with the Khilafat issue, it united Hindus and Muslims.",
       "Gandhiji's early satyagrahas were at Champaran (1917), Kheda (1918) and Ahmedabad (1918).",
       "After the violent Chauri Chaura incident (Gorakhpur) in February 1922, Gandhiji withdrew the Non-Cooperation Movement.",
       "The Dandi March began on 12 March 1930; the salt law was broken on 6 April — the Civil Disobedience Movement.",
       "The Lahore session of December 1929 passed the 'Purna Swaraj' resolution; 26 January 1930 was celebrated as Independence Day.",
       "The Gandhi-Irwin Pact was signed in 1931; the Poona Pact between Gandhiji and Ambedkar followed in 1932.",
       "The First World War (1914-1918) raised defence spending, so taxes and customs duties were increased and income tax was introduced.",
       "Villages were forcibly recruited for soldiers; crop failure in 1918-19 and the influenza epidemic killed lakhs of people.",
       "The Rowlatt Act (1919) allowed detention of political prisoners for 2 years without trial — 'no vakil, no appeal, no daleel'.",
       "The Khilafat Movement (1919) supported the Caliph of Turkey after its defeat in WWI; the Ali brothers (Muhammad Ali and Shaukat Ali) led it.",
       "The Non-Cooperation programme included boycott of foreign goods, government schools and colleges, courts and legislative councils, and adopting swadeshi and khadi.",
       "In Awadh (1920), Baba Ramchandra organised peasants against the exploitation of talukdars and zamindars.",
       "Alluri Sitarama Raju led a tribal revolt in the Gudem Hills of Andhra Pradesh; he was martyred in 1924.",
       "Plantation workers in Assam tried to leave the tea gardens for home in 1921, but the police caught and brutally beat them.",
       "In February 1922 at Chauri Chaura (Gorakhpur), an angry crowd set fire to the police station; several policemen died and Gandhiji immediately withdrew the movement.",
       "The Simon Commission (1928) had all-British members, so it was boycotted; slogans of 'Simon Go Back' rose and Lala Lajpat Rai died in a lathi charge.",
       "Gandhiji made salt the symbol of the movement because it touched the lives of rich and poor alike, and the salt tax was the most oppressive.",
       "The Civil Disobedience programme included breaking the salt law, boycotting foreign cloth and liquor, refusing land revenue, and violating forest laws.",
       "Thousands of women, including Kamaladevi Chattopadhyay, came out to make salt and picket liquor shops — the largest women's participation so far.",
       "The Congress boycotted the First Round Table Conference (November 1930, London); no decision without the Congress was meaningful.",
       "At the Karachi session (March 1931), the martyrdom of Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev was mourned and a resolution on Fundamental Rights was passed.",
       "Dalit participation remained limited; Dr. Ambedkar formed the Depressed Classes Association in 1930 and demanded separate electorates.",
       "Symbols of collective belonging emerged — Abanindranath Tagore's 'Bharat Mata' image (1905), Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay's 'Vande Mataram' (novel 'Anandamath', 1882) and Pingali Venkayya's tricolour flag."
      ],
