       [
        "Explain the concept of 'mayapuri'.",
        "'Mayapuri' means city of dreams — cities like Bombay and Calcutta that showed migrants dreams of success. Bombay realised this image through the film industry — glitter on screen, the cramped reality of chawls. Calcutta was 'mayapuri' through education and job opportunities. Ironically, most migrants found struggle, not dreams; yet every generation kept coming with new dreams. 'Mayapuri' is thus a symbol of both urban attraction and urban inequality.",
        "long"
       ],
       [
        "Review the effects of industrialisation on the social life of cities.",
        "Industrialisation changed the social fabric of cities. First — joint families broke, nuclear families and chawl culture came. Second — caste bonds loosened — all worked together in factories, though discrimination persisted. Third — women began working outside homes — laying the foundation of economic independence. Fourth — leisure got commercialised — pubs, cinema, sports. Fifth — class consciousness awoke — workers' organisations and strikes happened. Sixth — the village-city distance grew — migrants lived double lives. Thus industrialisation made cities laboratories of modernity — liberation and exploitation walked together.",
        "long"
       ],
       [
        "Compare the workers' movements of Bombay and London.",
        "In London the Chartist Movement (1838-48) was political — demanding the vote; later trade unions fought for wages and hours — the 1867/1884 reforms brought success. In Bombay movements were both economic and nationalist — mill workers' strikes in the 1920s; nationalist leadership also supported — but colonial repression was greater. Similarity — workers formed organisations in both places and used strikes as weapons. Difference — in London demands were met within a democratic framework; in Bombay the colonial government repressed — like the 1929 Meerut Conspiracy Case. Conclusion — contexts differed, the spirit of struggle was one.",
        "long"
       ],
       [
        "Comment on crime and policing in 19th-century cities.",
        "Poverty, unemployment and overcrowding increased theft and cheating in cities — Durgacharan Ray described Calcutta's 'swindlers'. London too had a big crime problem. In response modern policing developed — after London's 1829 Metropolitan Police, the system was strengthened; regular patrols, stations and crime records came up. In Bombay-Calcutta too colonial policing expanded — but there its main job was also to crush anti-government activity. Thus cities gave birth to modern law and order — security along with increased surveillance.",
        "long"
       ],
       [
        "Explain the role of colonialism in the rise of modern cities.",
        "Colonialism shaped modern cities in two ways. First — colonial loot (raw material, markets) accumulated capital in Europe, making metropolises like London flourish — dockyards were a product of colonial trade. Second — new cities like Bombay, Calcutta and Singapore were built in colonies — as harbours, railways and administrative centres; their planning served European interests — 'civil lines' versus 'native town'. Colonies supplied cheap labour and raw material; they were also markets for finished goods. Thus modern urbanisation was a product of the imperial economy — the glitter of metropolises hid the exploitation of colonies.",
        "long"
       ],
       [
        "Describe the public health challenges in cities.",
        "19th-century cities were a curse for public health. Crowded one-room houses, lack of clean water, open drains — cholera, typhoid and plague struck repeatedly. In London, Booth exposed the short lives of the poor in 1887; in Bombay the 1896 plague killed thousands. In response sanitation movements started — sewer systems, clean water supply and vaccination began. Bombay's 1898 Improvement Trust was a result of this. Lesson — town planning must include public health; otherwise cities become 'death traps'.",
        "long"
       ]
